This was [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]'s eighth participation in the UEFA Super Cup, winning it four times previously and finishing as runners-up on three previous occasions. It was [[Eintracht Frankfurt]]'s first UEFA Super Cup appearance.

==Venue==
[[File:Olympiastadion 2 2020-08-12.jpg|thumb|300px|The [[Helsinki Olympic Stadium|Olympic Stadium]] in [[Helsinki]] hosted the match.]]

The [[Helsinki Olympic Stadium]] was selected as the final host by the UEFA Executive Committee during their meeting in [[Amsterdam]], Netherlands on 2 March 2020.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025a-0f8e7599aae5-eab7d48ee1f2-1000--uefa-executive-committee-agenda-for-amsterdam-meeting/ |title=UEFA Executive Committee agenda for Amsterdam meeting |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=27 February 2020 |access-date=27 February 2020}}</ref> The [[Albanian Football Association]] also had bid for the match to be hosted in [[Tirana]], but withdrew prior to the vote, instead focusing on securing the [[2022 UEFA Europa Conference League final]].<ref name="ExCo"/>

The match was the first UEFA club competition final to be held in Finland. The stadium was previously used as a venue for the [[UEFA Women's Euro 2009]], where it hosted four group stage matches and the [[UEFA Women's Euro 2009 final|final]].<ref>{{cite news |title=UEFA Super Cup bound for Helsinki and Kazan |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/025b-0ed522ae0bce-a8e0f2fd983d-1000/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 March 2020 |access-date=2 March 2020 }}</ref>

==Pre-match==

===Officials===
On 3 August 2022, UEFA named English official [[Michael Oliver (referee)|Michael Oliver]] as the referee for the match. Oliver had been a [[FIFA International Referees List|FIFA referee]] since 2012, officiated at [[UEFA Euro 2020]] and was the referee for the [[2015 FIFA U-17 World Cup#Final|2015 FIFA U-17 World Cup final]]. He was joined by fellow countrymen Stuart Burt and Simon Bennett as assistant referees, while Donatas Rumšas of Lithuania served as the fourth official. Tomasz Kwiatkowski of Poland was selected as the [[video assistant referee]] (VAR), with compatriot [[Bartosz Frankowski]] and [[Tiago Martins (referee)|Tiago Martins]] of Portugal serving as the assistant VAR officials.<ref name="referees"/>

==Match==
===Summary===
[[Eintracht Frankfurt]] had the first chance in the match in the 14th minute with [[Thibaut Courtois]] saving in a one-on-one from [[Daichi Kamada]]. In the 36th minute [[Kevin Trapp]] got down to his left to save a low shot from [[Vinícius Júnior]]. From the resulting corner [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] went in front when [[Casemiro]] headed back from the end line on the right to [[David Alaba]] who tapped into the net from close range.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/aug/10/real-madrid-eintracht-frankfurt-uefa-super-cup-match-report|title=Real Madrid lift Super Cup after Alaba and Benzema sink Eintracht Frankfurt|work=The Guardian|date=10 August 2022|access-date=11 August 2022}}</ref> Trapp made another save in the 55th minute before Casemiro hit the crossbar from the edge of the box two minutes later. In the 65th minute Vinícius played the ball in from the left to [[Karim Benzema]] who made it 2–0 with a shot which goalkeeper [[Kevin Trapp]] failed to keep out.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/62475679|title=Real Madrid 2 Eintracht Frankfurt 0|work=BBC Sport|date=10 August 2022|access-date=11 August 2022}}</ref>

